@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# logredact
+
+Removes personal data from log files before they are shared.
+
+```
+python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Reads each `FILE` in the order given, or standard input when no `FILE` is
+given (`-` also means standard input), and writes every line to standard
+output with emails, IPv4 addresses and payment card numbers replaced.
+Everything else is passed through unchanged.
+
+Python 3.11+, standard library only.
+
+## Running
+
+The package lives in `src/`, so put that on `PYTHONPATH`:
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact /var/log/app.log > app.redacted.log
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count app.log 2> counts.txt
+cat app.log | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact
+```
+
+## What gets redacted
+
+| Input | Output |
+| --- | --- |
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` |
+| `192.168.0.10` | `[IP]` |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111`, `4111-1111-1111-1111`, `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` |
+
+- **Emails** — a local part, `@`, dot-separated domain labels and an
+  alphabetic top-level domain of two or more characters.
+- **IPv4** — four dot-separated numbers, each 0–255. Look-alikes are left
+  alone: `999.1.1.1`, `256.1.1.1`, `1.2.3.999`, `1.2.3`, `1.2.3.4.5`.
+- **Cards** — 13 to 19 digits, optionally in groups separated by *single*
+  spaces or *single* hyphens, that pass the Luhn checksum. Digit runs that
+  fail Luhn are left alone (`1234567890123`), as are runs that are too short
+  or too long (a 20-digit run is never a card).
+
+## Output
+
+`--count` prints a summary to standard error once all output has been
+written:
+
+```
+emails=3 ips=12 cards=1
+```
+
+Exit status is `0` normally. If a `FILE` cannot be read, the tool prints
+`logredact: <file>: <reason>` to standard error and exits `2`.
+
+## Behaviour worth knowing
+
+Choices made where the task left room:
+
+- **An unreadable file stops the run.** Files already processed have been
+  written to standard output, the failing file is named on standard error,
+  and the exit status is `2`; later files are not read, and no `--count`
+  summary is printed (output is incomplete, so the tallies would mislead).
+- **Lines are preserved byte for byte.** CRLF endings, a missing final
+  newline and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 all survive untouched
+  (decoding uses `surrogateescape`), so only the redacted spans ever change.
+- **Digits belonging to a dotted number are not card digits.** Both
+  `1.2.3.999` and `4111111111111111.00` are left alone rather than read as
+  card candidates, and a trailing sentence period still works:
+  `from 10.0.0.7.` → `from [IP].`.
+- **One digit run may hold several cards.** `4111111111111111 4111111111111111`
+  becomes `[CARD] [CARD]`. Within a run, candidates are matched
+  leftmost-first and as long as possible, at group boundaries only.
+- **Addresses win over their parts.** An email is matched before its digits
+  can be taken for an IP or a card, and an IP before its leading octet can
+  be absorbed into a digit run, so `12:30:45 192.168.0.1` redacts correctly.
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+src/logredact/__main__.py   python -m logredact
+src/logredact/cli.py        argument parsing, file/stdin handling, exit codes
+src/logredact/redact.py     patterns, Luhn check, counting
+tests/                      unit tests and subprocess-level CLI tests
+```
+
+`redact.py` is usable as a library:
+
+```python
+from logredact import Counts, redact
+
+counts = Counts()
+clean = redact("user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n", counts)
+print(clean, counts.summary())
+```
+
+## Tests
+
+```sh
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m unittest discover -s tests -t tests
+```
